# infobot, suppressions

Every `//nolint`, `# nosec` or equivalent in this repository, with the question
that was asked and the answer that was given. **A suppression that is not here
is a defect**, because the register is the only thing standing between a
silenced check and nobody remembering why.

## S-1, package-level tables Go cannot declare immutable

**Five marks, one rule, four files.** The two on the compiled rate tables in
`internal/pricing/pricing.go` went with the tables when the rate file became the
only source. The seven on the palette went when the colours became a value
passed down from `Main`, since palette.json overwrote them and they were not
read-only after all.

    internal/render/render_test.go       //nolint:gochecknoglobals    the fixed clock
    internal/state/state.go         ×2   //nolint:gochecknoglobals    the input key list, and the compiled schema
    internal/state/state_test.go         //nolint:gochecknoglobals    the fixed timestamp
    internal/usage/usage.go              //nolint:gochecknoglobals    the counter names

**The rule is "no package-level mutable state" and none of these is mutable.**
Every one is built once at initialisation and only read afterwards: the keys an
input payload may carry, a `sync.OnceValues` that compiles the status schema on
first use, the names of the counters, and two fixed values that make a test
deterministic.

**Go has no way to say so.** `const` reaches strings, numbers and booleans, and
none of these is one: they are maps, slices and structs. So the language offers
package-level `var` and nothing else, and the linter reads the only construct
available as the thing it warns about. That is the gap being registered, and
not a disagreement about whether mutable global state is bad.

**Why marks rather than a config change.** toolbox's shared golangci
configuration already scopes this linter off for `styles?.go`, on the argument
that Lip Gloss styles are conventionally package-level and confining them to
their own file is good practice independently. These five are not styles: they
are data tables and test fixtures sitting beside the code that uses them, where
moving them to a file named for the linter would be arranging the source to
suit a tool.

Widening a shared configuration reaches five other repositories on the strength
of one repository's findings, and none of them has met this yet. So it is marked
here, where the argument is, and if a second Go project arrives with the same
shape the config is the right place to revisit.

**What would retire this entry.** Go gaining immutable composite declarations,
which is not close; or these tables moving behind accessor functions, which
would trade a linter finding for indirection nobody reading the code wants.

Line numbers are deliberately not recorded. They go stale on the next edit and a
stale line number is worse than none. `grep -rn nolint internal/` is the current
list and it is one command.

## S-2, gosec on the shims, the width probe and the transcript reads

**Ten marks, four rules, four files.**

    cmd/statusline/shim_test.go  ×3   //nolint:gosec    writing an executable shim
    cmd/statusline/shim_test.go  ×2   //nolint:gosec    running the shim it just wrote
    internal/render/width.go          //nolint:gosec    asking the terminal its width
    internal/state/state.go           //nolint:gosec    creating the state directory
    internal/usage/usage.go      ×3   //nolint:gosec    reading a transcript by path

**G306, a file written 0755.** `shim_test.go` writes a shell script and then
runs it. A shim that is not executable is not a shim, so 0600 would test
something other than the thing. The file is in a per-test temporary directory
that goes away with the test.

**G204, a subprocess built from a variable.** Two are the same test running the
shim whose path it just chose. The third is `width.go` asking the terminal how
wide it is; the argv comes from this repository's own configuration and not from
a payload, and the alternative to running a command is not knowing the width.

**G301, a directory created 0755.** `~/.local/state/infobot` holds status files
that silo's board reads, so the directory is deliberately readable. Nothing in
it is a secret: it carries a session id, a model name, a working directory and
four numbers.

**G304, a file opened by a path from a variable.** All three read a Claude Code
transcript whose path arrives in the hook payload. That is the input this program
exists to read, and gosec is right that it is variable and wrong that it is
avoidable: there is no fixed path a transcript lives at.

**What would retire these.** For G204 in `width.go`, a terminal-width syscall
rather than a subprocess. For the rest, nothing: each is the operation the code
is for, and the mark records that somebody looked rather than that nobody did.

## S-4, gosec on the config read and the winsize ioctl

**Two marks, two rules, two files.**

    internal/render/layout_config.go   //nolint:gosec    reading palette.json or layout.json
    internal/render/width_tty.go       //nolint:gosec    the kernel writing a winsize

Asked 2026-09-30, when the suppression check found both marks in the source and
in no entry: register them, remove them, or refactor them out? Answered
2026-09-30: register both, with the reasons below.

**G304, a file opened by a path from a variable.** `readConfig` opens
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/infobot/<name>`, and the name is one of two string literals
this package passes. Both configuration files go through this one read, so one
mark covers them.

**G103, unsafe.** TIOCGWINSZ takes a pointer the kernel writes the window size
through, and `syscall.Syscall` takes it as a `uintptr`. That conversion is what
the ioctl is.

**What would retire these.** For G103, `golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize`,
at the price of a dependency. For G304, nothing: reading a configured file is
the operation.

